{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2021 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "96beecd2d599816c20093b305175ee89b4a7133711f287d80719c9ee1c2ff140" :edb/db-before-t 2020 :edb/db-after-t 2021 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535333 50 #inst "2026-10-08T22:17:59.556Z" 13194139535333 true] [17592186344229 1100 "The triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nis defined over the type I region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2},\\,-2\\le y\\le 4,\\,0\\le z\\le 1+xy}.\n$$\n\nUse $[a,b]$ as the full nonempty projection of the exactly specified region $R$ onto the $y$ axis. The inner limits may be piecewise. What is $a+b?$" 13194139535333 true] [17592186344229 1100 "The triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nis defined over the type I region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2},\\,-2\\le y\\le 4,\\,0\\le z\\le 1+xy}.\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b?$" 13194139535333 false] [17592186344229 1145 "A type I region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{u}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {u}_{2}(x,y)}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062541/assets/1bd4b6d0b1aa955921c1f7855d3fbd95417072a67504d9de9be44dfbbed592e5.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type I region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z]\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x,y)=0,\\,{u}_{2}(x,y)=1+xy.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1+xy}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA.\n$$\n\nNow, the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2},\\,-2\\le y\\le 4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by expressing the double integral over $D$ as a repeated integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1+xz}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA & ={\\int }_{-2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{1+xy}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dx\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1+xy}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=-2+4=2.\n$$" 13194139535333 false] [17592186344229 1145 "The inequalities defining $R$ are retained exactly. For every $y\\in[-2,4]$, choosing $x=0$ leaves the nonempty fiber $0\\le z\\le1$. Thus the full nonempty $y$ projection is $[a,b]=[-2,4]$ and $a+b=2$.\n\nThe base must also satisfy $1+xy\\ge0$. When $y<0$, this requires $x\\le-1/y$. The bounds $y^2$ and $-1/y$ meet at $y=-1$. Hence\n\n$$\\iiint_R f\\,dV=\\int_{-2}^{-1}\\int_0^{-1/y}\\int_0^{1+xy}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy+\\int_{-1}^4\\int_0^{y^2}\\int_0^{1+xy}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy.$$\n\nOn the first branch the $x$ cap is $-1/y$; on the second it is $y^2$. The source solution used $x\\le y^2$ throughout and therefore included reversed $z$ bounds for some negative $y$. Its isolated expression $1+xz$ is also a transcription error: the upper $z$ bound is $1+xy$. These solution errors do not change the correct endpoint sum $2$." 13194139535333 true]] :edb/tempids {}}
